{"study_id": "zk26_seeded_fold2_v3_haiku55_repeat2_20261106", "search_seed": 42, "factor_id": "7393a7565806741a0e53684c607a79ef6c0efdb090aca4b31d4c7e7cd0dd01ac", "expression": "SIGN(RETURN($adj_close@1d, 1.0))", "canonical_expression": {"op": "SIGN", "args": [{"op": "RETURN", "args": [{"op": "field", "args": [], "value": "adj_close@1d"}, {"op": "const", "args": [], "value": 1.0}], "value": null}], "value": null}, "description": "Daily speculative churn, measured as daily traded volume relative to the open interest it turns over, predicts the next 12 five-minute log returns with the sign of the day's price move. Churn is z-scored over 20 daily bars and multiplied by SIGN(1-day return). Under the reversal account the raw sign is negative: high churn after a daily rise predicts a lower next-hour return, and high churn after a daily fall predicts a higher one. The primary factor should beat both churn alone and the volume-only interaction on train and validation, which would separate churn from ordinary abnormal volume.", "direction": -1, "origins": [{"source": "v3_async", "trial": 70, "attempt": 70, "run_id": "futures_kernel_5m_full-all-fold2-v3_async-42-27dcf84074"}]}
{"study_id": "zk26_seeded_fold2_v3_haiku55_repeat2_20261106", "search_seed": 42, "factor_id": "9230a67d88b4fa223dd7110fb4eb8603591ec3c4ab0486cd10da857ea0ffda15", "expression": "SUBTRACT(DIVIDE($adj_close@1d, TS_MAX($adj_close@1d, 250.0)), 1.0)", "canonical_expression": {"op": "SUBTRACT", "args": [{"op": "DIVIDE", "args": [{"op": "field", "args": [], "value": "adj_close@1d"}, {"op": "TS_MAX", "args": [{"op": "field", "args": [], "value": "adj_close@1d"}, {"op": "const", "args": [], "value": 250.0}], "value": null}], "value": null}, {"op": "const", "args": [], "value": 1.0}], "value": null}, "description": "Investors anchor on the multi-quarter high as a reference price. When adj_close is close to its trailing 250-trading-day high, they underreact to good news, so the price drifts up over the next hour. When it is far below the high, the drift is weaker. The mechanism predicts a positive raw IC for proximity, defined as adj_close@1d divided by its 250-day maximum, minus one.", "direction": -1, "origins": [{"source": "v3_async", "trial": 123, "attempt": 123, "run_id": "futures_kernel_5m_full-all-fold2-v3_async-42-27dcf84074"}]}
{"study_id": "zk26_seeded_fold2_v3_haiku55_repeat2_20261106", "search_seed": 42, "factor_id": "a933b365018d1d65d61057f5ea5a0a4c46ab9a41704da26ba979dddcdb16ed0c", "expression": "RETURN($adj_close@1d, 5.0)", "canonical_expression": {"op": "RETURN", "args": [{"op": "field", "args": [], "value": "adj_close@1d"}, {"op": "const", "args": [], "value": 5.0}], "value": null}, "description": "Efficiency of the 5-day daily adj_close path, measured as the 5-day net simple return divided by the sum of absolute 1-day simple returns, predicts the next 12-bar 5m adj_close log return. Directional flow sustained across several sessions should continue. Choppy multi-day paths with large gross movement and small net change reflect liquidity-provider churn and should partly revert. The sign is set by the Train primary IC. Trial:45 (12-bar intraday efficiency) gave same-sign train and validation IC of about 0.004 and 0.0036, just below the 0.005 gate, which motivates testing the same mechanism at the daily span. The daily version is kept distinct from trial:50 (intraday, no daily band) and from the admitted trend member trial:33 (daily trend gated by open-interest sign).", "direction": -1, "origins": [{"source": "v3_async", "trial": 129, "attempt": 129, "run_id": "futures_kernel_5m_full-all-fold2-v3_async-42-27dcf84074"}]}
