{"study_id": "zk26_seeded_fold2_v3_haiku55_repeat2_20261106", "search_seed": 42, "factor_id": "9d61279e4813bf7d503661774e8566978f76046eb08d00fd8adc352b14b1b6db", "expression": "SUBTRACT(DIVIDE($adj_close@1d, TS_MIN($adj_close@1d, 250.0)), 1.0)", "canonical_expression": {"op": "SUBTRACT", "args": [{"op": "DIVIDE", "args": [{"op": "field", "args": [], "value": "adj_close@1d"}, {"op": "TS_MIN", "args": [{"op": "field", "args": [], "value": "adj_close@1d"}, {"op": "const", "args": [], "value": 250.0}], "value": null}], "value": null}, {"op": "const", "args": [], "value": 1.0}], "value": null}, "description": "Investors anchor on the trailing 250-trading-day low as a reference price. Holders of losing positions near that low resist selling (disposition effect), which slows the absorption of bad news, so prices near the low drift lower over the next 12 five-minute bars. The primary factor is low_prox = adj_close@1d / TS_MIN(adj_close@1d, 250) - 1, with its raw sign kept. A positive raw IC means higher values, farther above the low, precede higher returns, which is the mirror of the admitted high-side factor (trial 123, raw IC negative on train and validation). If the low-side raw sign matches trial 123, the effect is a generic range-position reversal rather than anchoring specific to the low.", "direction": -1, "origins": [{"source": "v3_async", "trial": 143, "attempt": 143, "run_id": "futures_kernel_5m_full-all-fold2-v3_async-42-27dcf84074"}]}
